To force a text field in Adobe Acrobat (using JavaScript) to display its content in columns, you need to understand that standard form fields (like text fields) do not support multi-column layout natively. However, you can simulate a columnar display by formatting the text content with line breaks or tabs.

Here’s a basic example of how you can use JavaScript in Adobe Acrobat to format the content of a text field to appear in two columns:

### Example: Format Text into Two Columns

```javascript
// Get the text field
var field = this.getField("YourTextFieldName");

// Sample data (could be dynamic)
var data = [
  { col1: "Item 1", col2: "Value A" },
  { col1: "Item 2", col2: "Value B" },
  { col1: "Item 3", col2: "Value C" }
];

// Format the data into a two-column string
var formattedText = "";
for (var i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {
  formattedText += data[i].col1 + "\t" + data[i].col2 + "\n";
}

// Set the formatted text to the field
field.value = formattedText;
```

### Notes:
- This script uses a tab (`\t`) to separate columns and a newline (`\n`) to move to the next row.
- The appearance of the columns depends on the font and the width of the text field.
- If you need a more precise layout (e.g., fixed-width columns), consider using a PDF form with multiple text fields or using a rich text field with HTML-like formatting (limited in Acrobat JavaScript).

