I can provide you with a basic Swift UI app template to get you started. Here's a simple example of a SwiftUI app that displays a welcome message and a button that toggles a message when pressed.

### Step 1: Create a new SwiftUI project in Xcode

1. Open Xcode.
2. Go to **File > New > Project**.
3. Select **App** under the iOS tab.
4. Choose **SwiftUI** as the interface and **Swift** as the language.
5. Name your project and click **Create**.

### Step 2: Replace the default code with the following

```swift
import SwiftUI

@main
struct MyFirstApp: App {
    var body: some Scene {
        WindowGroup {
            ContentView()
        }
    }
}

struct ContentView: View {
    @State private var showGreeting = false

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text("Welcome to My App!")
                .font(.largeTitle)
                .padding()

            if showGreeting {
                Text("Hello, SwiftUI!")
                    .font(.title)
                    .foregroundColor(.blue)
            }

            Button(action: {
                showGreeting.toggle()
            }) {
                Text("Toggle Greeting")
                    .padding()
                    .background(Color.green)
                    .foregroundColor(.white)
                    .cornerRadius(8)
            }
        }
        .padding()
    }
}
```

### Step 3: Run the app

1. Select a simulator or connect a device.
2. Click the **Run** button (▶️) in Xcode.

This app will display a welcome message and a button. When you press the button, it will toggle the display of a greeting message.

